'''altered''' (âl′tėrd), ''p. a.'' Changed; different. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In ''geol.'', metamorphosed: applied to a rock of which the constituent minerals have been changed by chemical action subsequently to its formation or deposition. Rocks are commonly rendered harder and more crystalline by such alteration. When softening of crystalline rocks takes place, it is usually accompanied by hydratation, or the taking up of water. (''b'') In ''mineral.'', applied to a mineral whose substance has been changed either chemically or molecularly, as a garnet altered to chlorite or aragonite altered to calcite. (''c'') Castrated.}}

'''alter ego''' (al′tėr ē′gō). [L.: ''alter'', other, second; ''ego'' = E. ''I'': see ''alter'' and ''ego''.] Second self; another self; counterpart; double. {{smaller|Sometimes applied as a title to a person who has full powers to act for another, as in the case of a Spanish viceroy when exercising regal power.}}

'''alter idem''' (al′tėr ī′dem). [L.: ''alter'', other (see ''alter''); ''idem'', the same.] Another and the same; another precisely similar.

'''alterity''' (al-ter′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [< ML. ''alterita(t-)s'', < L. ''alter'', other: see ''alter''.] The state or quality of being other or different. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Your outness is but the feeling of otherness (''alterity'') rendered intuitive, or ''alterity'' visually represented.
{{right|''Coleridge'', Notes on Shakspere, II. 295.}}}}

